print guideline. Kept as ONE logical change — a single mechanical pass over one surface (log.Error call sites) with one audit and one lint-rule introduction; splitting by controller tier would duplicate the audit and fragment the lint rule. **Re-split trigger**: if the E002 call-site audit reveals materially more call sites than the 75%-of-calls estimate implies, split by controller tier (e.g., DSCI + DSC + component controllers vs module + service controllers) along the audited call-site counts.\n- **Scope boundary ambiguity (scope trap, mild)**: Out-of-Scope excludes \"custom log dashboards or alerting rules\" while the backwards-compatibility NFR requires identifying and updating text-based alert rules that break. Interpretation: authoring new dashboards/alerts is excluded; updating existing SRE-owned rules identified by E004 is coordination/remediation that may fall outside this strategy's delivery scope — flag for PM validation.\n- **DSCI query breakage**: existing Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on Request.Name/DSCInitialization break at migration; mitigation is single-release transition + release-notes documentation (scoped in the docs epic). \n- **Process gates**: all Prerequisites & Process Gates are \"Pending human review\" (boilerplate) — strategy-level gates, tracked outside this decomposition.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable as a dependency-gated tier split: the only external dependency with uncertain timing is COO team adoption (Loki/Tempo operators), which is voluntary per platform policy overlay 0008 and gates none of this strategy's deliverables — publication (not adoption) is the deliverable. The controller-runtime logr dependency is internal and exists. Manager (E002) and cloudmanager (E003) conversions deliver independent partial value and are already separate epics that can be scheduled independently per the release-timing decision.\n\n---\n\nNow the epic files.\n\n**E001:**\n\nTitle: Resolve structured log field convention scope\n\nDe