les HLR1-HLR7 (field-set scope); no HLR directly satisfied.\" That's honest.\n\nFrontmatter: epic_id, title, parent_strat, component=AI Core Platform, team=ODH Operator team, type=Investigation, priority=P0, dependencies=[] (nothing), investigation_signals: question_specificity=1, source_accessibility=0, local_runnability=0, cluster_hardware_dependence=0, human_judgment_required=-2.\n\nWait — dependencies for E001: empty. Does anything gate E001? No. ✓.\n\n**E002:**\n\nTitle: Convert manager binary reconciliation error logs to structured resource context\n\nDescription: Converts all reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator manager binary from message-embedded resource identity to structured key-value pairs (name, namespace, resourceKind — camelCase), across all five controller tiers and all 15 action files, including migrating the DSCInitialization controller from its non-standard key names (Request.Name, DSCInitialization) to the standard convention. Applies the field set confirmed by the structured log field convention scope decision. Also introduces the CI lint rule that enforces the convention going forward.\n\nScope:\n- Audit all log.Error() call sites in manager-binary reconciliation error paths; track coverage in a checklist (this also confirms the audited share of call sites embedding resource identity, reported by SME input as ~75%).\n- Convert every call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string to structured KV pairs, applying the resolved convention field set.\n- Add resourceKind to reconciliation error logs in: the DataScienceCluster controller and the 8 component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI); the module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator); the DSCInitialization controller; and the 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup).\n- Migrate DSCI structured keys from Request.Name/DSCInitialization to name/namespace.\n- Cover all 15 action files across 4 tiers: 8 component action files (components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go), 3 service action files (services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go), 1 DSC action file (datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go), 3 module action files (modules/modules_controller_actions*.go).\n- Handle cluster-scoped resources (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization): emit name and resourceKind, with namespace omitted or empty — never a spurious value.\n- Add a CI lint/static-analysis rule flagging log.Error calls that embed name/namespace in message text or omit required structured fields.\n- Risks: single-release DSCI transition to avoid long-term key inconsistency; coordinate with SRE for alert-rule impact (consolidated validation workstream); overage note + re-split trigger.\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- CI lint rule passes with zero violations on the manager binary: no log.Error call in a reconciliation error path embeds resource name/namespace in message text, and required structured fields are present.\n- Unit tests assert reconciliation error logs across all five controller tiers and all 15 action files contain name, namespace (where the resource is namespaced), and resourceKind with correct values.\n- DSCI error logs use the standard camelCase field names; a codebase grep confirms no remaining references to Request.Name or DSCInitialization as structured log keys.\n- Cluster-scoped resources emit name and resourceKind with namespace omitted or empty — unit tests confirm no spurious namespace value.\n- The call-site audit checklist is complete, covering every log.Error call site in manager reconciliation error paths.\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR1 (manager portion), HLR2, HLR3, HLR4, HLR6, HLR7 (bundled P1 — resourceKind on DSC + component controllers is added in the same edit as the P0 identity conversion at the same call sites; not separately schedulable).\n\nFrontmatter: type=Implementation, priority=P0, component=AI Core Platform, team=ODH Operator team, dependencies=[RHAISTRAT-8010-E001], gated_by=RHAISTRAT-8010-E001, gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite, gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach=\"If the convention-scope decision adopts fields beyond name/namespace/resourceKind, apply the extended field set at the same call sites — the conversion pattern is rewritten, not the epic structure.\", ai_signals: change_specificity=1, pattern_precedent=1, adapter_pattern=0, existing_foundation=1, open_questions=-1, external_dependency=1, human_process_gates=0, repo_access=1, architecture_claims=1.\n\n**E003:**\n\nTitle: Convert cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs to structured resource context\n\nDescription: Applies the same structured resource-context conversion to the cloudmanager binary's multi-cloud infrastructure controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine). These controllers use the shared reconciler pattern and already receive name/namespace from controller-runtime's request logger; the work converts any message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs and adds resourceKind. Independently schedulable from the manager-binary conversion (separate binary; the same-release-vs-subsequent-release decision is a PM scheduling call).\n\nScope:\n- Audit log.Error() call sites in cloudmanager reconciliation error paths.\n- Convert message-embedded resource identity to structured KV pairs per the resolved convention field set.\n- Add resourceKind to all three cloudmanager controllers' reconciliation error logs.\n- Note: cloudmanager deploys only on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave) — impact scope narrower than manager.\n- Risks/Notes: release timing (same release as manager vs subsequent) is an open PM decision; this epic is independently schedulable and does not block manager-binary work.\n\nAcceptan