ity Operator components — Loki Operator, Tempo Operator — on a voluntary basis per platform policy overlay 0008). Includes the DSCI key-migration guide with before/after query examples and the release-notes documentation of the migration.\n\nScope (content outline inside Scope):\n- Contribution guideline specifying: required fields (name, namespace, resourceKind per the resolved convention), camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, missing resourceKind) with before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs.\n- DSCI key-name migration guide: release-notes documentation with before/after query examples for operators using Request.Name/DSCInitialization-based queries; single-release transition note.\n- Publication for external operator adoption: publish the convention as a specification; file tracking issues in the COO projects (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator); adoption is at their discretion per platform policy.\n- Design doc content (three-problem diagnosis, before/after examples, migration guide) per the strategy's supporting-documentation commitment.\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- The contribution guideline exists, specifies the required fields and camelCase rationale, documents the three-problem diagnosis with before/after code examples, and is technically reviewed against the implemented conversions (field list and examples match the shipped code).\n- The DSCI migration guide provides before/after query examples and is included in release-notes documentation.\n- The convention is published for external adoption and tracking issues are filed in the COO projects; external adoption itself is voluntary and not a completion condition.\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR8.\n\nFrontmatter: type=Implementation, implementation_type=docs-authoring, priority=P1, dependencies=[E001, E002, E003, E004], gated_by=E001, gate_failure_impact rewrite (\"If the resolved convention includ