{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c85699ebceb1583","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8010 — Operator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context\n11\t\n12\t## Triage Assessment\n13\t\n14\tThe strategy was **not** triaged as below-threshold despite S-size and single-component scope, because both escape conditions fire:\n15\t\n16\t1. **Genuine unknowns**: the strategy's open questions include (a) the structured-log field-set scope — whether the convention includes fields beyond `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` — whose answer changes what every conversion epic does (the field set applied at each call site), and (b) which existing log-based alerts/dashboards rely on message-string patterns that will change, which the strategy mandates be identified during a log-consumer regression validation step before merge. Both are handled as Investigation epics (E001, E004).\n17\t2. **Multiple distinct work streams**: code implementation across the operator's two binaries (mechanical Go log-call conversion) plus substantive documentation authoring (a new contribution guideline documenting the convention, a DSCI key-migration guide, and publication for external operator adoption — HLR8). These are architecturally distinct work products warranting separate PR review cycles.\n18\t\n19\tNot docs-only: rhods-operator requires code changes.\n20\t\n21\t## Epic List\n22\t\n23\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n24\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Resolve structured log field convention scope | Investigation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Convert manager binary reconciliation error logs to structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Convert cloudmanager binary reconciliation error logs to structured resource context | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E004 | Validate log consumer compatibility and audit alert-rule impact | Investigation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E005 | Document and publish the structured log convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n30\t\n31\t## Dependency DAG\n32\t\n33\t```mermaid\n34\tgraph TD\n35\tE001[\"E001 Resolve convention field scope (Inv)\"] --> E002[\"E002 Manager binary conversion (Impl)\"]\n36\tE001 --> E003[\"E003 Cloudmanager binary conversion (Impl)\"]\n37\tE002 --> E004[\"E004 Log consumer / alert-rule validation (Inv)\"]\n38\tE003 --> E004\n39\tE001 --> E005[\"E005 Document + publish convention (docs)\"]\n40\tE002 --> E005\n41\tE003 --> E005\n42\tE004 --> E005\n43\t```\n44\t\n45\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E004 → E005 (length 4).\n46\t\n47\t## DAG Justification\n48\t\n49\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n50\t|------|------|-----------|\n51\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The convention-scope decision determines the field set applied at every manager-binary call site; resolving it first means each call site is touched exactly once. True gate with `rewrite` impact: if additional fields are adopted, the conversion pattern (not the epic structure) is rewritten. |\n52\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | Same field-set gate applies to the cloudmanager-binary conversion. |\n53\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 + Rule 11 | The guideline documents the resolved field set; its content is directly gated by the decision (also transitively reachable via E002/E003, listed explicitly because the doc's primary content is the convention itself). |\n54\t| E002 → E004 | Rules 3/4 (validation needs the built artifact) | The log-consumer regression validation requires a converted test build to exercise before/after output; its outcome may reveal problems requiring remediation (`add_remediation`). |\n55\t| E003 → E004 | Rules 3/4 | The consolidated validation covers both binaries' log output and both consumer families (Loki on OpenShift, CloudWatch on cloud deployments). |\n56\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The guideline describes what was built. |\n57\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same — docs blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n58\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 + content dependency | The DSCI key-migration guide and its before/after query examples are finalized from the validation results. |\n59\t| E002 ∥ E003 (no edge) | Rules 14/15 | Different binaries (different areas of the same repo), no shared artifacts — parallel; merge-conflict coordination risk only, not a dependency. |\n60\t| E002 ∥ E004 start? No — E004 after both | Rule 3 | E004 is a downstream verification, not an upstream gate; it does not change what gets built, but its failure mode (ingestion breakage, broken alert queries) requires remediation before merge, so it gates the docs epic and merge readiness, not the conversions' existence. |\n61\t\n62\tNo conditional branch files were generated for E001: its bounded outcomes (minimal three-field convention vs extended field set) do **not** change downstream epic structure — the same epics exist either way, only the field set applied at the same call sites differs — so the conditionality is recorded as `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` on E002/E003/E005 rather than as duplicated branch epics.\n63\t\n64\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n65\t\n66\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n67\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n68\t| HLR1 — reconciliation error logs embedding name/namespace in message strings converted to structured KV pairs | P0 | E002, E003 | Manager binary portion in E002; cloudmanager portion in E003 |\n69\t| HLR2 — DSCI controller migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase keys | P0 | E002 | Single-release transition; migration guide documented in E005 |\n70\t| HLR3 — service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | |\n71\t| HLR4 — action file error logs include `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` across all 4 tiers (15 files) | P0 | E002 | 8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module action files |\n72\t| HLR5 — cloudmanager controllers (AWS/Azure/CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) error logs include `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` | P0 | E003 | |\n73\t| HLR6 — module controller error logs (8 module handlers) include `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | |\n74\t| HLR7 — DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P1 | E002 | **bundled** — not separately schedulable: `resourceKind` on the DSC and component controllers is added during the same log-call edit as the P0 identity conversion (HLR1) at the same call sites in the same files; a separate epic would double-touch every call site and build the same surface twice. The P1 label reads as relative value (queryability enhancement — kind is implied by controller name), not a deferral signal. |\n75\t| HLR8 — document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | P1 | E005 | docs-authoring; priority per strategy frontmatter (Major → P1) |\n76\t\n77\tNFR coverage: the backwards-compatibility NFR (log ingestion + alert rules) is verified by E004, with unit-test-level ACs on E002/E003; the performance NFR requires no verification (controller-runtime logr is production-proven — architecture context confirms logr usage throughout the controller hierarchy). All 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n78\t\n79\t## Open Questions Disposition\n80\t\n81\t| Open question | Disposition |\n82\t|---------------|-------------|\n83\t| Should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind (attempt count, error category)? | **E001 Investigation** — gates E002/E003/E005. Bounded outcome (2 possibilities) that changes the field set, not epic structure, so it is recorded as a `rewrite` gate rather than conditional branch epics. |\n84\t| Which existing log-based alerts/dashboards rely on message-string patterns that will change? | **E004 Investigation** — the strategy mandates identification during the log-consumer regression validation step before merge; E004 produces the inventory and validation evidence. |\n85\t| Should cloudmanager be prioritized for the same release as manager, or follow in a subsequent release? | Scheduling decision (PM) — does not change which epics exist or what they do. E003 is independently schedulable; recorded as a health warning, not a DAG edge or epic. |\n86\t| What is the actual percentage of log calls embedding resource identity? | Informational — the audit is the first step of E002's scope with a coverage-checklist AC; conversion proceeds the same way regardless of the percentage (convert all embedded call sites). Not an Investigation. |\n87\t| (SME-resolved) How many component action files exist? | Resolved by Staff Engineer input: all 8 components have exactly one action file; 15 total across 4 tiers. No epic needed; counts baked into E002 scope. |\n88\t\n89\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n90\t\n91\tStaff Engineer / SME input is non-empty and takes precedence over the AI-generated Technical Approach. Overrides applied:\n92\t\n93\t- **Controller architecture corrected to 5 tiers** (DSC; 8 component controllers; module controller with 8 module handlers; DSCI; 5 service controllers) — the AI draft described four tiers and omitted the module controller. E002 scope uses the SME-confirmed 5-tier architecture.\n94\t- **Action-file count corrected**: \"~16 component action files\" is wrong and mislabeled; exactly 15 action files across 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module). E002 scope uses the confirmed 15-file breakdown.\n95\t- **camelCase field naming** (`name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind`) per controller-runtime convention — applied as the field standard across all epics.\n96\t- **cloudmanager uses the shared reconciler** and already gets name/namespace — E003 scope is resourceKind + embedded-identity conversion only.\n97\t- **External operator treatment** (voluntary adoption per platform policy overlay 0008, confirmed by RFE approval comment) — no epic generated for COO; publication scoped in E005.\n98\t- **Cluster-scoped-resource AC** (namespace omitted or empty for DataScienceCluster/DSCInitialization) — carried as an AC on E002.\n99\t- The former open question premised on \"~16 component action files\" is answered (8/8) and removed from the unknowns list.\n100\t\n101\t## Component Mapping\n102\t\n103\tThe strategy names one component: **rhods-operator** (manager + cloudmanager binaries). The canonical RHAI component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) has no literal \"rhods-operator\"; the closest canonical name is **AI Core Platform** — rhods-operator is the RHOAI platform root / central control plane, and the \"AI Core\" family maps the core platform services (cf. `AI Core Dashboard` for odh-dashboard, `AI Core Platform Security` for platform security work). All five epics are assigned `AI Core Platform`; this non-obvious mapping is logged here per the component-name constraint. The Cluster Observability Operator team is a **passive recipient** (receives the published convention; adoption at their discretion per platform policy overlay 0008) — no validation epic generated for them because they validate nothing; the publication deliverable is scoped in E005.\n104\t\n105\t## Health Warnings\n106\t\n107\t- **Release-timing decision pending (OQ3)**: whether the cloudmanager conversion ships in the same release as the manager conversion is an open PM decision. E003 is independently schedulable and blocks nothing upstream; if deferred, the cloudmanager portion of the consolidated validation (E004) and the publication timing follow the release plan. Not a priority inversion — HLR5 is P0 whenever scheduled.\n108\t- **Effort-label vs item-sum tension**: the strategy labels effort S (1-2 sprints) but its itemized estimate sums to ~2-2.25 sprints (0.5 audit + 0.75-1 conversion + 0.25 cloudmanager + 0.5 docs/lint). Managed by splitting the two binaries into separate epics and the docs into its own epic; the residual tension is a strategy-level estimate inconsistency, flagged for PM validation.\n109\t- **E002 overage + re-split trigger**: the manager-binary conversion (audit + conversion) is estimated at ~1.25-1.5 sprints, above the one-sprint guideline. Kept as ONE logical change — a single mechanical pass over one surface (log.Error call sites in the manager binary) with one audit and one lint-rule introduction; splitting by controller tier would duplicate the audit and fragment the lint rule. **Re-split trigger**: if the E002 call-site audit reveals materially more embedded-identity call sites than the ~75%-of-calls estimate implies, split by controller tier (e.g., DSCI + DSC + component controllers vs module + service controllers) along the audited per-tier call-site counts.\n110\t- **Scope boundary ambiguity (mild scope trap)**: Out-of-Scope excludes \"custom log dashboards or alerting rules\" while the backwards-compatibility NFR requires identifying and updating text-based alert rules that break. Interpretation applied: authoring new dashboards/alerts is excluded; updating existing SRE-owned rules identified by E004 is coordination/remediation that may fall outside this strategy's delivery scope — flagged for PM validation.\n111\t- **DSCI query breakage**: existing Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` break at migration. Mitigation: single-release transition + release-notes documentation with before/after query examples (scoped in E005).\n112\t- **Process gates boilerplate**: all Prerequisites & Process Gates are \"Pending human review\" — strategy-level gates tracked outside this decomposition; E005 carries the documentation-review dependency.\n113\t- No priority inversions detected: P0/P1 assignments follow HLR labels; the only P1 implementation epic (E005, docs-authoring) derives its priority from the strategy frontmatter per the docs-authoring priority exception.\n114\t\n115\t## Tiered Delivery\n116\t\n117\tNot applicable as a dependency-gated Tier 1/Tier 2 split. The only external dependency with uncertain timing is COO team adoption (Loki/Tempo operators), which is **voluntary** per platform policy overlay 0008 and gates none of this strategy's deliverables — the deliverable is publication (E005), not adoption. The controller-runtime logr dependency is internal and already exists. Manager (E002) and cloudmanager (E003) conversions deliver independent partial value and are separate, independently schedulable epics per the release-timing decision, which is the scheduling flexibility the strategy's third open question asks about.\n118\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bbd21d00a17c8887","agent_id":"a75f8bdcfb2bb8afa","session_id":"5fd65945-afc1-4d94-9de3-0f90915cbf9f","uuid":"bf7f1115-f448-4a56-9414-0ef6f8b964b0","timestamp":"2026-10-23T18:26:36.075Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c85699ebceb1583","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
