ator team (correct — the strategy assigns both binaries and the docs to that team; the COO team is a passive recipient with no deliverable). Splits follow the endorsed axes: binary boundary (E002 manager vs E003 cloudmanager) and work-product type (E005 pure content authoring; E002/E003 pure code; no code/content mixing). No scope duplication: E002 and E003 touch disjoint binaries, and the CI lint rule is authored once (E002) and referenced by AC in E003 rather than rebuilt. The manager-binary epic (E002) exceeds the one-sprint guideline (~1.25-1.5 sprints) but is one logical change — a single mechanical pass over one surface with one audit and one lint-rule introduction — and the decomposition records a concrete re-split trigger in Health Warnings (split by controller tier along audited per-tier call-site counts if the audit reveals materially more embedded-identity call sites than the ~75% estimate implies). Per the oversized-epic rule this is a managed watch item, not a defect, and no split is suggested here.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nBoth Investigations are genuine. E001's outcome (minimal vs extended field set) changes what every downstream epic does — a true gate, correctly recorded as `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` with a populated fallback on E002, E003, and E005, and every `gated_by` target is in the epic's direct `dependencies`. E004 is a legitimate validation Investigation (resolves the strategy's alert-rule open question; its failure mode requires remediation before merge) with downstream dependent E005. E004 itself lists no Investigation dependencies, so no gate is required on it. The audit portion of E002 is correctly kept inside the Implementation (the summary's disposition explains why it is not an Investigation: conversion proceeds identically regardless of the audited percentage). One minor: E005 lists Investigation E004 in `dependencies` while its gate metadata covers only E001. The summary's own justification supports t