{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-90b7cf6d7f6d4af1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: E003 AC relies on the CI lint rule authored in E002 while E002 and\n10\t    E003 are correctly parallel with no edge (Rules 14/15); the AC-level coupling\n11\t    can only be verified at merge time once E002 rule lands - note the merge-time\n12\t    verification in E003 so the parallel scheduling is not misread as E003 being independently\n13\t    verifiable\n14\t- severity: minor\n15\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n16\t  description: E005 lists Investigation E004 in dependencies while gate metadata (gated_by/gate_failure_impact)\n17\t    covers only the E001 gate; the summary characterizes the E004 edge as content\n18\t    finalization (not an upstream gate) and gated_by is single-valued, but automated\n19\t    gate detection may infer a gate from the Investigation-to-Implementation edge\n20\t    - label the E004 dependency explicitly as a content-input/docs-last edge in E005\n21\t    body or the DAG justification\n22\terror: null\n23\t---\n24\t## Review Summary\n25\t\n26\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n27\tRecommendation: accept\n28\t\n29\tContract validator: OK (skills/epic-decompose/scripts/validate_decomposition.py exits 0 — body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, and implementation-type parallelism all pass). Triage consistency: `triage: null` with 5 epics — full decomposition, multi-epic output expected, no contradiction.\n30\t\n31\tThis decomposition correctly navigates the strategy's fine-grained structure: all 8 HLRs are mapped with the P1/P0 bundling analysis recorded in the traceability matrix row (HLR7, with the double-touch rationale the rule requires), the oversized manager-binary epic carries a concrete re-split trigger in Health Warnings, the externally-controlled environment preconditions are placed in Risks/Notes rather than acceptance criteria, and the decision to use a `rewrite` gate instead of conditional branch files for E001 matches the branch rule's \"outcomes that change downstream structure\" qualifier. Two minor clarity nits are recorded below; neither affects execution.\n32\t\n33\t## Criterion Details\n34\t\n35\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n36\tAll 8 HLRs map to epics: HLR1 split correctly across E002 (manager) and E003 (cloudmanager); HLR2/3/4/6 to E002; HLR5 to E003; HLR8 to E005. Traceability matrix present in the summary. Priority inheritance is sound: E001 (P0) blocks only P0 work; E005 is docs-authoring and correctly derives P1 from the strategy's Major priority per the docs-authoring exemption. The one cross-priority epic (E002 maps HLR1-HLR6 at P0 plus HLR7 at P1) is a justified bundle with the analysis recorded exactly where the mapping is read: the matrix row is flagged `bundled` with the rationale that `resourceKind` on DSC/component controllers is added in the same log-call edit at the same call sites as the HLR1 identity conversion, and a split would double-touch every call site — the mirror-case analysis the rule requires. No unanalyzed priority collapse; no P0 HLR missing.\n37\t\n38\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n39\tAll 8 edges in the mermaid diagram match the target epics' frontmatter `dependencies` exactly, in both directions; arrows follow the dependency→dependent convention; no cycles; no references to nonexistent epics; no branch files exist (correctly — see Completeness). Parallelism: E002 ∥ E003 is correct per Rules 14/15 (different binaries, no shared build-against artifacts), and no Rule 7/8/10 pairs exist (no konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics). Rule 11 edges (E002→E005, E003→E005) are present and correctly do not trigger priority inheritance. The E001→E002/E003 gates are justified true gates (field set determines what every call site applies); E002/E003→E004 is a genuine artifact dependency (the validation needs the converted test build). Critical path 4 (E001→E002→E004→E005) is above the S heuristic but is forced by the strategy's own mandates — the field-set open question must be resolved first, the backwards-compatibility NFR mandates before-merge validation, and HLR8 mandates docs — and the summary documents the S-label vs item-sum tension (~2-2.25 sprints, effectively M-standard range 3-4). One minor: E003's lint-rule AC relies on the CI lint rule authored in E002's scope while the epics are correctly parallel with no edge; the AC-level coupling is the rule-compliant shape (Rule 25 pattern) but can only be verified at merge time once E002's rule lands — worth an explicit note so the parallel scheduling is not misread as E003 being independently verifiable.\n40\t\n41\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n42\tAll epics are AI Core Platform / ODH Operator team (correct — the strategy assigns both binaries and the docs to that team; the COO team is a passive recipient with no deliverable). Splits follow the endorsed axes: binary boundary (E002 manager vs E003 cloudmanager) and work-product type (E005 pure content authoring; E002/E003 pure code; no code/content mixing). No scope duplication: E002 and E003 touch disjoint binaries, and the CI lint rule is authored once (E002) and referenced by AC in E003 rather than rebuilt. The manager-binary epic (E002) exceeds the one-sprint guideline (~1.25-1.5 sprints) but is one logical change — a single mechanical pass over one surface with one audit and one lint-rule introduction — and the decomposition records a concrete re-split trigger in Health Warnings (split by controller tier along audited per-tier call-site counts if the audit reveals materially more embedded-identity call sites than the ~75% estimate implies). Per the oversized-epic rule this is a managed watch item, not a defect, and no split is suggested here.\n43\t\n44\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n45\tBoth Investigations are genuine. E001's outcome (minimal vs extended field set) changes what every downstream epic does — a true gate, correctly recorded as `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` with a populated fallback on E002, E003, and E005, and every `gated_by` target is in the epic's direct `dependencies`. E004 is a legitimate validation Investigation (resolves the strategy's alert-rule open question; its failure mode requires remediation before merge) with downstream dependent E005. E004 itself lists no Investigation dependencies, so no gate is required on it. The audit portion of E002 is correctly kept inside the Implementation (the summary's disposition explains why it is not an Investigation: conversion proceeds identically regardless of the audited percentage). One minor: E005 lists Investigation E004 in `dependencies` while its gate metadata covers only E001. The summary's own justification supports the non-gate reading — E004 is \"not an upstream gate\" and the edge exists so the migration guide is \"finalized from the validation results\" (content input, docs-last ordering), and `gated_by` is single-valued so only the scope-determining gate (E001) can be recorded — but an automated consumer scanning an Investigation→Implementation edge may infer a gate; the relationship should be labeled explicitly as a content-input edge (or the E004 failure branch folded into the gate record).\n46\t\n47\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n48\tType-appropriate signal fields throughout: E001/E004 carry `investigation_signals`; E002/E003/E005 carry `ai_signals`; all five `-ai-signals.md` rationale files exist and their values match the frontmatter. Investigation signals check out against the rubric: E001 `human_judgment_required: -2` (the gating question is itself a PM/Observability product decision) and `source_accessibility: 0` (the answer does not yet exist in any readable artifact); E004 `cluster_hardware_dependence: -2` (the gating ingestion question requires a live cluster with the production consumer stack) and `local_runnability: 0`. Implementation signals are consistent: `open_questions: -1` on E002/E003/E005 is correct (the field-set question would change the approach at every call site and is unresolved at decomposition time); `architecture_claims: 1` is properly calibrated — the strategy makes architecture claims (controller tiers, action-file counts, logr foundation), the architecture-context file covers rhods-operator, and the rationales cite `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md` (which exists and corroborates the logr/controller-runtime foundation, the cloudmanager binary and its three infrastructure controllers, and the DSCInitialization controller), including an honest note reconciling the context file's \"three tiers\" summary with the SME's five-tier correction. E005's `pattern_precedent: 0` (no existing convention guideline confirmed) and `human_process_gates: -1` (technical review plus documentation review) are justified. Computed score fields were not re-derived, per instructions.\n49\t\n50\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n51\tEvery epic has testable ACs. Rule-mandated ACs: E005 (docs-authoring) has the \"technically reviewed against the implemented conversions\" AC (Rule 24); no konflux chain exists (Rule 25 N/A); the strategy is a log-format refactor, not a capability replacement, so rollback/feature-flag ACs are N/A — the strategy's own mitigation (single-release DSCI transition, migration guide, release notes, and the E004 pre-merge validation) covers the regression risk. The soft-AC trap is handled correctly: dev-cluster and log-consumer-stack availability is recorded in E004's Risks/Notes, not as an AC, and E005 explicitly scopes voluntary COO adoption out. E002's ACs are concretely testable (lint zero violations, unit tests across all tiers and 15 action files, DSCI key grep, cluster-scoped namespace